uct.\n\n3. **Grid Method (Vertical Multiplication):**\n   - Breaks down each number into its tens and ones place values and multiplies each part separately.\n   - Adds the products of the partial products to get the final result.\n\n4. **Partial Products Method:**\n   - Breaks down each number into its tens and ones place values and multiplies each part separately.\n   - Adds the partial products to get the final result.\n\n5. **Box Method (Horizontal Multiplication):**\n   - Breaks down each number into its tens and ones place values and multiplies each part separately.\n   - Adds the products of the partial products to get the final result.\n\n6. **Standard Long Multiplication Method:**\n   - Breaks down each number into its tens and ones place values and multiplies each part separately.\n   - Adds the products of the partial products to get the final result.\n\nAll these methods ultimately lead to the same final answer of 5544. <|User|>Thank you for the summary. Can you provide an example of how to use the area model method to solve a different multiplication problem, say 34 and 52? <｜Assistant｜> <think>\nCertainly! Let's use the area model method to solve the multiplication problem of 34 and 52. Here's how we can set it up:\n\n1. **Break down the numbers:**\n   - 34 can be broken down into 30 and 4.\n   - 52 can be broken down into 50 and 2.\n\n2. **Set up the area model:**\n   - Draw a 2x2 grid and label the top row with 30 and 4, and the left column with 50 and 2.\n\n```\n  30 |  4\n× 50 |  2\n-----\n 1500|  60\n-----\n 1600|  80\n-----\n 1720\n```\n\n3. **Calculate the areas of the smaller rectangles:**\n   - The area of the rectangle formed by 30 and 50 is:\n     ```\n     30 × 50 = 1500\n     ```\n   - The area of the rectangle formed by 30 and 2 is:\n     ```\n     30 × 2 = 60\n     ```\n   - The area of the rectangle formed by 4 and 50 is:\n     ```\n     4 × 50 = 200\n     ```\n   - The area of the rectangle formed by 4 and 2 is:\n     ```\n     4 × 2 = 