n2. Multiply \\(50 \\times 3\\) to get 150.\n3. Multiply \\(1 \\times 70\\) to get 70.\n4. Multiply \\(1 \\times 3\\) to get 3.\n5. Group \\(3500\\) and \\(150\\) together.\n6. Group \\(70\\) and \\(3\\) together.\n7. Add the grouped partial products: \\(3500 + 150 = 3650\\).\n8. Add the results of the grouped partial products: \\(3650 + 73 = 3723\\).\n\nThus, the final answer is \\(\\boxed{3723}\\). <|User|>The different approach using the partial products method is clear. Can you also show me how to solve this using the standard long multiplication method again, but this time with a different breakdown? Please reason step by step.<｜Assistant｜> <think>\nCertainly! Let's solve \\(51 \\times 73\\) using the standard long multiplication method again, but this time with a different breakdown. We'll break down the multiplication into smaller steps and use a different way of grouping the partial products:\n\n1. **Set up the multiplication:**\n   ```\n   51\n× 73\n```\n\n2. **Multiply 51 by 3 (ones digit of 73):**\n   - Write down the result below the line, aligning the digits by place value.\n   ```\n   51\n× 73\n-----\n  153\n   ```\n\n3. **Multiply 51 by 70 (tens digit of 73, which is 7 × 10):**\n   - Shift the result one place to the left (because we're multiplying by 10).\n   ```\n   51\n× 73\n-----\n  153\n+3570\n   ```\n\n4. **Add the two results together:**\n   - Align the numbers by place value and add them.\n   ```\n   51\n× 73\n-----\n  153\n+3570\n-----\n 3723\n   ```\n\nSo, the product of 51 and 73 is \\(3723\\).\n\nTo summarize the detailed steps:\n1. Multiply 51 by 3 (ones digit of 73) to get 153.\n2. Multiply 51 by 70 (tens digit of 73, which is 7 × 10) to get 3570.\n3. Add the two results: 153 + 3570 = 3723.\n\nThus, the final answer is \\(\\boxed{3723}\\). <|User|>The different breakdown in the standard long multiplication method is clear. Can you also show me how to solve this using the area model for multiplication again, but this time with a different