e other and add them:\n     ```\n     72\n   ×  34\n   -----\n     288\n   +2160\n   -----\n     2448\n     ```\n\n5. **The final product is**:\n   ```\n   72\n   × 34\n   -----\n   2448\n   ```\n\nSo, using the standard algorithm, the product of 72 and 34 is 2448. <|User|>Can you also show me how to solve this problem using the partial products method? <｜Assistant｜> <think>\nCertainly! Let's solve the multiplication problem \\(72 \\times 34\\) using the partial products method:\n\n1. **Break down the numbers into their place values**:\n   - \\(72\\) can be broken down into \\(70 + 2\\).\n   - \\(34\\) can be broken down into \\(30 + 4\\).\n\n2. **Multiply each part of the first number by each part of the second number**:\n   - \\(70 \\times 30\\)\n   - \\(70 \\times 4\\)\n   - \\(2 \\times 30\\)\n   - \\(2 \\times 4\\)\n\n3. **Calculate each partial product**:\n   - \\(70 \\times 30 = 2100\\)\n   - \\(70 \\times 4 = 280\\)\n   - \\(2 \\times 30 = 60\\)\n   - \\(2 \\times 4 = 8\\)\n\n4. **Sum the partial products**:\n   - Add all the partial products together:\n     \\[\n     2100 + 280 + 60 + 8 = 2448\n     \\]\n\nSo, using the partial products method, the product of 72 and 34 is 2448. <|User|>Can you also show me how to solve this problem using the grid method? <｜Assistant｜> <think>\nCertainly! Let's solve the multiplication problem \\(72 \\times 34\\) using the grid method:\n\n1. **Set up the grid**:\n   - Write the digits of 72 along the top of the grid and the digits of 34 along the left side of the grid.\n   ```\n   7 | 2\n   ───┼───\n   3 | 4\n   ```\n\n2. **Multiply each digit in the top row by each digit in the left column**:\n   - Multiply 7 by 3, 7 by 4, 2 by 3, and 2 by 4.\n   ```\n   7 | 2\n   ───┼───\n   3 | 4\n   7 | 8\n   2 | 6\n   2 | 8\n   ```\n\n3. **Write the results in the grid**:\n   - Place the products in the corresponding cells of the grid.\n   ```\n   7 | 2\n   ───┼───\n   3 | 4\n   7 | 8\n   2 | 6\n   2 | 8\n   ```\n\n4. **Add the numbers 