   ×  9\n   -----\n     6120\n     ```\n\n4. **Add the two results together**:\n   - Write the results of the two multiplications one below the other and add them:\n     ```\n     68\n   ×  97\n   -----\n     476\n   +6120\n   -----\n     6596\n     ```\n\n5. **The final product is**:\n   ```\n   68\n   × 97\n   -----\n   6596\n   ```\n\nSo, using the standard algorithm, the product of 68 and 97 is 6596. <|User|>Can you also show me how to solve this problem using the partial products method? <｜Assistant｜> <think>\nCertainly! Let's solve the multiplication problem \\(68 \\times 97\\) using the partial products method:\n\n1. **Break down the numbers into their place values**:\n   - \\(68\\) can be broken down into \\(60 + 8\\).\n   - \\(97\\) can be broken down into \\(90 + 7\\).\n\n2. **Multiply each part of the first number by each part of the second number**:\n   - \\(60 \\times 90\\)\n   - \\(60 \\times 7\\)\n   - \\(8 \\times 90\\)\n   - \\(8 \\times 7\\)\n\n3. **Calculate each partial product**:\n   - \\(60 \\times 90 = 5400\\)\n   - \\(60 \\times 7 = 420\\)\n   - \\(8 \\times 90 = 720\\)\n   - \\(8 \\times 7 = 56\\)\n\n4. **Sum the partial products**:\n   - Add all the partial products together:\n     \\[\n     5400 + 420 + 720 + 56 = 6596\n     \\]\n\nSo, using the partial products method, the product of 68 and 97 is 6596. <|User|>Can you also show me how to solve this problem using the grid method? <｜Assistant｜> <think>\nCertainly! Let's solve the multiplication problem \\(68 \\times 97\\) using the grid method:\n\n1. **Set up the grid**:\n   - Write the digits of 68 along the top of the grid and the digits of 97 along the left side of the grid.\n   ```\n   6 | 8\n   ───┼───\n   9 | 7\n   ```\n\n2. **Multiply each digit in the top row by each digit in the left column**:\n   - Multiply 6 by 9, 6 by 7, 8 by 9, and 8 by 7.\n   ```\n   6 | 8\n   ───┼───\n   9 | 7\n   6 | 56\n   8 | 72\n   8 | 56\n   ```\n\n3. **Write the results in the grid**:\n   - Place the products in the corresponding cells of the grid.\n   ```\n   6 | 8\n   ───┼───\n   9 | 7\n   6 | 56\n   8 | 72\n   8 | 56\n   ```\n\n4. **Add the numbers in each diagonal**:\n   - Start from the bottom left and move diagonally to the top right, adding the numbers in each diagonal.\n   - If the sum is a two-digit number, carry over the tens digit to the next diagonal.\n   ```\n   6 | 8\n   ───┼───\n   9 | 7\n   6 | 56\n   8 | 72\n   8 | 56\n   ───┼───\n   560 | 560\n   ```\n\n5. **Read the final product from the top left to the bottom right**:\n   - The final product is 6596.\n\nSo, using the grid method, the product of 68 and 97 is 6596. <|User|>Can you also show me how to solve this problem using the standard algorithm for multiplication, but this time with a different number, say 56 and 78? <｜Assistant｜> <think>\nCertainly! Let's solve the multiplication problem \\(56 \\times 78\\) using the standard algorithm:\n\n1. **Write the numbers in a vertical format**:\n   ```\n   56\n× 78\n```\n\n2. **Multiply 56 by the units digit of 78 (which is 8)**:\n   - First, multiply 56 by 8:\n     ```\n     56\n   ×  8\n   -----\n     448\n     ```\n   - Write 448 below the line, aligning the digits by place value.\n\n3. **Multiply 56 by the tens digit of 78 (which is 7), and remember to shift one place to the left**:\n   - First, multiply 56 by 7:\n     ```\n     56\n   ×  7\n   -----\n     392\n     ```\n   - Since we are multiplying by the tens place, we shift the result one place to the left:\n     ```\n     56\n   ×  7\n   -----\n     3920\n     ```\n\n4. **Add the two results together**:\n   - Write the results of the two multiplications one below the other and add them:\n     ```\n     56\n   ×  78\n   -----\n     448\n   +3920\n   -----\n     4368\n     ```\n\n5. **The final product is**:\n   ```\n   56\n   × 78\n   -----\n   4368\n   ```\n\nSo, using the standard algorithm, the product of 56 and 78 is 4368. <|User|>Can you also show me how to solve this problem using the partial products method? <｜Assistant｜> <think>\nCertainly! Let's solve the multiplication problem \\(56 \\times 78\\) using the partial products method:\n\n1. **Break down the numbers into their place values**:\n   - \\(56\\) can be broken down into \\(50 + 6\\).\n   - \\(78\\) can be broken down into \\(70 + 8\\).\n\n2. **Multiply each part of the first number by each part of the second number**:\n   - \\(50 \\times 70\\)\n   - \\(50 \\times 8\\)\n   - \\(6 \\times 70\\)\n   - \\(6 \\times 8\\)\n\n3. **Calculate each partial product**:\n   - \\(50 \\times 70 = 3500\\)\n   - \\(50 \\times 8 = 400\\)\n   - \\(6 \\times 70 = 420\\)\n   - \\(6 \\times 8 = 48\\)\n\n4. **Sum the partial products**:\n   - Add all the partial products together:\n     \\[\n     3500 + 400 + 420 + 48 = 4368\n     \\]\n\nSo, using the partial products method, the product of 56 and 78 is 4368. <|User|>Can you also show me how to solve this problem using the grid method? <｜Assistant｜> <think>\nCertainly! Let's solve the multiplication problem \\(56 \\times 78\\) using the grid method:\n\n1. **Set up the grid**:\n   - Write the digits of 56 along the top of the grid and the digits of 78 along the left side of the grid.\n   ```\n   5 | 6\n   ───┼───\n   7 | 8\n   ```\n\n2. **Multiply each digit in the top row by each digit in the left column**:\n   - Multiply 5 by 7, 5 by 8, 6 by 7, and 6 by 8.\n   ```\n   5 | 6\n   ───┼───\n   7 | 8\n   5 | 48\n   6 | 42\n   6 | 48\n   ```\n\n3. **Write the results in the grid**:\n   - Place the products in the corresponding cells of the grid.\n   ```\n   5 | 6\n   ───┼───\n   7 | 8\n   5 | 48\n   6 | 42\n   6 | 48\n   ```\n\n4. **Add the numbers in each diagonal**:\n   - Start from the bottom left and move diagonally to the top right, adding the numbers in each diagonal.\n   - If the sum is a two-digit number, carry over the tens digit to the next diagonal.\n   ```\n   5 | 6\n   ───┼───\n   7 | 8\n   5 | 48\n   6 | 42\n   6 | 48\n   ───┼───\n   420 | 480\n   ```