      "text": " <think>\nTo solve \\(94 \\times 67\\) using the lattice method, follow these steps:\n\n1. **Set up the lattice grid**: Draw a 2x2 grid because we are multiplying a two-digit number by a two-digit number.\n2. **Write the numbers on the top and left edges of the grid**:\n   ```\n     9 | 4\n   6 | 7\n   ```\n3. **Divide each cell in the grid into two triangles** by drawing a diagonal line.\n4. **Multiply the digits at the top and left of each cell** and write the result in the corresponding cell, with the tens digit in the upper triangle and the units digit in the lower triangle:\n   ```\n     9 | 4\n   6 | 7\n   ---------\n   54 | 36\n   42 | 28\n   ```\n5. **Add the numbers in each diagonal column**, starting from the bottom right:\n   - In the first diagonal (from bottom right to top left), add 36 and 28:\n     ```\n     64\n     ```\n   - In the second diagonal, add 54 and 42:\n     ```\n     96\n     ```\n6. **Combine the results** from the diagonals to get the final product:\n   ```\n     64\n   96\n   ```\n   So, the final product is 6298.\n\nTherefore, using the lattice method, we again arrive at the same result: the product of 94 and 67 is 6298. <|User|>Can you explain how to solve this problem using the grid method? <\uff5cAssistant\uff5c> <think>\nCertainly! The grid method, also known as the box method, is a visual and systematic way to multiply two numbers by breaking them down into their place values and then multiplying each part separately. Let's solve \\(94 \\times 67\\) using the grid method:\n\n1. **Break down the numbers into their place values**:\n   - 94 can be written as \\(90 + 4\\)\n   - 67 can be written as \\(60 + 7\\)\n\n2. **Set up the grid**:\n   ```\n   | 90 | 4\n   |____|____\n   60 |   |   |\n   7  |   |   |\n   ```\n\n3. **Multiply each part of the first number by each part of the second number** and fill in the grid:\n   - \\(90 \\times 60 = 5400\\)\n   - \\(90 \\times 7 = 630\\)\n   - \\(4 \\times 60 = 240\\)\n   - \