ultiple components, the area model can be used to break down the calculation. For instance, if a product is made up of 39 units, each with an area of 73 square meters, the total area can be calculated using the area model as:\n     \\[\n     39 \\times 73 = 39 \\times (70 + 3) = 39 \\times 70 + 39 \\times 3\n     \\]\n   - **Advantage:** It provides a clear visual representation of the multiplication, making it easier to understand the relationship between the dimensions and the total area.\n\n3. **Construction and Architecture:**\n   - **Example:** In construction, when calculating the total area of a building that is divided into multiple sections, the area model can be used to break down the calculation. For instance, if a building is 39 meters by 73 meters, the total area can be calculated using the area model as:\n     \\[\n     39 \\times 73 = 39 \\times (70 + 3) = 39 \\times 70 + 39 \\times 3\n     \\]\n   - **Advantage:** It provides a clear visual representation of the multiplication, making it easier to understand the relationship between the dimensions and the total area.\n\n4. **Education and Tutoring:**\n   - **Example:** In education, when teaching students how to calculate the area of a rectangle, the area model can be used as a step-by-step approach to help students understand the process. For instance, when teaching students how to calculate the area of a rectangle with dimensions 39 meters by 73 meters, they can use the area model as:\n     \\[\n     \\text{Area} = 39 \\times 73 = 39 \\times (70 + 3) = 39 \\times 70 + 39 \\times 3\n     \\]\n   - **Advantage:** It provides a clear visual representation of the multiplication, making it easier to understand the relationship between the dimensions and the area.\n\n5. **Financial Planning:**\n   - **Example:** In financial planning, when calculating the total area of a plot of land that is divided into multiple sections, the area model can be used to break down the calculation. For instance, if a perso