e where the distributive property is used in a financial context, like calculating the total cost of a subscription with a monthly fee and a one-time setup fee, but this time with a different monthly fee and a different setup fee?\n<\uff5cAssistant\uff5c>Certainly! Let's consider an example where you are subscribing to a service with a different monthly fee and a one-time setup fee. Suppose the monthly fee is $170 and the one-time setup fee is $1100. You want to calculate the total cost for 12 months.\n\nThe total cost \\(C\\) can be calculated using the formula:\n\\[ C = \\text{Monthly Fee} \\times \\text{Number of Months} + \\text{Setup Fee} \\]\n\nSubstituting the given values:\n\\[ C = 170 \\times 12 + 1100 \\]\n\nUsing the distributive property, we can break down the calculation:\n\\[ C = 170 \\times (12 + 0) + 1100 \\]\n\\[ C = 170 \\times 12 + 170 \\times 0 + 1100 \\]\n\\[ C = 2040 + 0 + 1100 \\]\n\\[ C = 3140 \\]\n\nSo, the total cost for 12 months is $3140.\n\nThis example demonstrates how the distributive property can be used to simplify the calculation of the total cost when dealing with a different monthly fee and a one-time setup fee. The property allows us to break down the calculation into simpler steps, making it easier to understand and compute.\n\nIf you have any more questions or need further examples, feel free to ask! <|User|>Thank you for the detailed examples. Can you give me one more example where the distributive property is used in a financial context, like calculating the total cost of a service with a base fee and a variable fee based on usage, but this time with a different base fee and a different variable fee?\n<\uff5cAssistant\uff5c>Certainly! Let's consider an example where you are using a service that has a different base fee and a variable fee based on the amount of usage. Suppose the base fee is $185 and the variable fee is $15 per unit of usage. You plan to use 1300 units of the service.\n\nThe total cost \\(C\\) can be calculate