e problem \\(82 \\times 61\\), we can break it down as follows:\n\\[82 \\times 61 = 82 \\times (60 + 1) = (82 \\times 60) + (82 \\times 1)\\]\n\nFirst, calculate \\(82 \\times 60\\):\n\\[82 \\times 60 = 4920\\]\n\nNext, calculate \\(82 \\times 1\\):\n\\[82 \\times 1 = 82\\]\n\nNow, add these two results together:\n\\[4920 + 82 = 5002\\]\n\nSince the sum of \\(4920 + 82\\) is indeed 5002, we can confirm that our original multiplication was correct. This method provides a way to verify the answer without needing to perform the multiplication again from scratch.\n\nAnother method is to use a calculator or perform the multiplication again to ensure accuracy. If you have a calculator, you can simply enter \\(82 \\times 61\\) and compare the result with 5002.\n\nBoth methods can be used to verify the correctness of the answer. <|User|>That's very helpful. Could you also explain how to use the distributive property in a more complex example, like \\(123 \\times 45\\)?\n</\uff5cAssistant\uff5c>\n<think>\nCertainly! Let's use the distributive property to break down the multiplication of \\(123 \\times 45\\). The distributive property allows us to split one of the numbers into parts that are easier to multiply. We can express 45 as \\(40 + 5\\), and then distribute the multiplication over these parts.\n\nSo, we have:\n\\[123 \\times 45 = 123 \\times (40 + 5)\\]\n\nUsing the distributive property:\n\\[123 \\times (40 + 5) = (123 \\times 40) + (123 \\times 5)\\]\n\nNow, let's calculate each part separately:\n1. Calculate \\(123 \\times 40\\):\n\\[123 \\times 40 = 4920\\]\n\n2. Calculate \\(123 \\times 5\\):\n\\[123 \\times 5 = 615\\]\n\nNow, add these two results together:\n\\[4920 + 615 = 5535\\]\n\nSo, using the distributive property, we find that:\n\\[123 \\times 45 = 5535\\]\n\nThis method can be applied to more complex numbers by breaking them down into simpler components. <\uff5cAssistant\uff5c>\n<answer>\nTo use the distributive property to solve \\(123 \\times 45\\), we